#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186091976 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "15b6b8a0-8e4b-51b5-b992-659f82730241" :knowledge-point/title "Converting from Cartesian Coordinates to Polar Coordinates: First or Fourth Quadrant"}] :question/id #uuid "d5dc3dd6-6366-5cb9-9deb-7f4c71de0bba" :question/math-academy-id "e-5077" :question/problem "Express the Cartesian point $P (1,-\\sqrt{3})$ in polar coordinates." :question/worked-solution "The first thing to realize is that the point $P$ lies in the fourth quadrant.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/935/Source/Images/353b325d00952160420a21721fdb8c2c.png)\n\nFirst, we determine $r$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} r &= \\sqrt{x^2 + y^2} \\\\[3pt] &= \\sqrt{1^2 + (-\\sqrt{3})^2} \\\\[3pt] &=\\sqrt{1+3} \\\\[3pt] &= 2 \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe then compute the reference angle $\\theta_R$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} \\theta_R &= \\left\\begin{vmatrix}\\arctan\\left(\\dfrac y x\\right)\\right & \\\\ &= \\left & \\arctan\\left(\\dfrac{-\\sqrt{3}}{1}\\right)\\right & \\\\ &= \\left & \\arctan\\left(-\\sqrt 3\\right)\\right & \\\\ &= \\left & -\\dfrac{\\pi}{3}\\right\\end{vmatrix} \\\\ &= \\dfrac{\\pi}{3} \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the point lies in the fourth quadrant, we subtract the reference angle from $2\\pi$, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nθ &= 2π - θ_{R} \\\\\n&= 2π - \\frac{π}{3} \\\\\n&= \\frac{5π}{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the polar coordinates of $P$ are\n\n$$\n\\left(2, \\dfrac{5\\pi}{3}\\right)\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186091977 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "49bf1f94-d992-511d-9016-d471f2ad4358" :knowledge-point/title "Converting from Cartesian Coordinates to Polar Coordinates for a Point on an Axis"}] :question/id #uuid "51309671-3a82-582f-93b0-e30c49dcaf7c" :question/math-academy-id "e-5078" :question/problem "Express the Cartesian point $P (0,-7)$ in polar coordinates." :question/worked-solution "First, we determine $r$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} r &= \\sqrt{0^2 + (-7)^2} \\\\ &= \\sqrt{7^2} \\\\ &= 7 \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the point $P$ lies on the negative half of the $y$-axis, we have\n\n$$\n\\theta=\\dfrac{3\\pi}{2}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the polar coordinates of $P$ are\n\n$$\n\\left(7, \\dfrac{3\\pi}{2}\\right)\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186091978 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6b04084c-8e69-5f53-a95d-664aba54aec9" :knowledge-point/title "Converting from Cartesian Coordinates to Polar Coordinates: Second or Third Quadrant"}] :question/id #uuid "54f6db85-a16b-5e8c-bd8d-caccc6f3563e" :question/math-academy-id "e-5079" :question/problem "Express the Cartesian point $P (-6, 8)$ in polar coordinates. Round your answer to three decimal places where appropriate." :question/worked-solution "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/935/Source/Images/40b1c3db885180812cfbf4edd958ae6a.png)\n\nFirst, we determine $r$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} r &= \\sqrt{x^2 + y^2} \\\\[3pt] &= \\sqrt{(-6)^2 + 8^2} \\\\[3pt] &=\\sqrt{100} \\\\[3pt] &= 10 \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the reference angle, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} \\theta_R &= \\left\\begin{vmatrix}\\arctan\\left(\\dfrac y x\\right)\\right & \\\\[3pt] &= \\left & \\arctan\\left(-\\dfrac 8{6}\\right)\\right\\end{vmatrix} \\\\[3pt] &\\approx 0.927\\,29. \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the point $P$ lies in the second quadrant, we subtract the reference angle from $\\pi$, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nθ &= π - θ_{R} \\\\\n&= π - 0.92729 \\\\\n&\\approx 2.21429 \\\\\n&\\approx 2.214\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nto three decimal places. Therefore, the polar coordinates of $P$ are $(10,2.214)$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265545 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6b04084c-8e69-5f53-a95d-664aba54aec9" :knowledge-point/title "Converting from Cartesian Coordinates to Polar Coordinates: Second or Third Quadrant"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1c34a24a-fa7d-50aa-a762-1482b5f93c89" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2,\\frac{7\\pi }{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265547} {:answer/id #uuid "16104b08-06e5-52b9-a0fe-c4c4181a8cf6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(4,\\frac{5\\pi }{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265548} {:answer/id #uuid "03cd0c5a-10d5-54a6-8d62-efb2e2890119" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2,\\frac{3\\pi }{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265549} {:answer/id #uuid "79e1054d-10f7-5bfd-a52c-d202465210a3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\sqrt{2},\\frac{5\\pi }{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265550} {:answer/id #uuid "5a84080d-aba0-5196-b03d-d2f566a3266e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\sqrt{2},\\frac{\\pi }{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265551}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "03cd0c5a-10d5-54a6-8d62-efb2e2890119" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2,\\frac{3\\pi }{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265549} :answer-field/id #uuid "a1e134b5-07c0-5f81-94e0-47c53e19ddbb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265546}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3b0fc0f8-a46c-5367-a119-f6e07e143535" :question/math-academy-id "q-120234" :question/problem "A point $P$ has Cartesian coordinates $(-\\sqrt{2},\\sqrt{2}).$ What are the polar coordinates of $P?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we determine $r:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-\\sqrt{2})^{2}+(\\sqrt{2})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2+2} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4} \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the reference angle, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\theta }_{R} & =|\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{y}{x})| \\\\  & =|\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{-\\sqrt{2}})| \\\\  & =|-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(1)| \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the point $P$ lies in the second quadrant, we subtract the reference angle from $\\pi ,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\theta  & =\\pi -{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & =\\pi -\\frac{\\pi }{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\pi }{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the polar coordinates of $P$ are  $(2,\\frac{3\\pi }{4}).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265552 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6b04084c-8e69-5f53-a95d-664aba54aec9" :knowledge-point/title "Converting from Cartesian Coordinates to Polar Coordinates: Second or Third Quadrant"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ec2fac4d-7b3b-5357-ab91-6729951fd566" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2\\text{ }\\,a,2.8)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265554} {:answer/id #uuid "d0d7c192-00bc-506e-8c98-b7ae7305d523" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\sqrt{5}\\text{ }\\,a,4.6)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265555} {:answer/id #uuid "39f37820-66f3-54dd-8f9f-0af939d663b0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\sqrt{2}\\text{ }\\,a,3.5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265556} {:answer/id #uuid "9e5e7b94-33ad-53b9-8245-650eba67e6e0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\sqrt{5}\\text{ }\\,a,2.7)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265557} {:answer/id #uuid "e59b7642-35de-50de-8ef4-fd84ba716083" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(5\\text{ }\\,a,2.6)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265558}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9e5e7b94-33ad-53b9-8245-650eba67e6e0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\sqrt{5}\\text{ }\\,a,2.7)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265557} :answer-field/id #uuid "e9424b8a-d167-5e4a-ae9d-353e879197ec" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265553}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "dbd80e6e-d392-5f34-a56c-e530095045aa" :question/math-academy-id "q-120241" :question/problem "A point $P$ has Cartesian coordinates $(-2a,a),$ where $a$ is a positive real number. What are the polar coordinates of $P?$  Round your answer to the nearest tenth where appropriate." :question/worked-solution "First, we determine $r:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-2a)^{2}+{a}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5{a}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5}\\text{ }\\,a.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the reference angle, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\theta }_{R} & =|\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{y}{x})| \\\\  & =|\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{a}{-2a})| \\\\  & =|-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{1}{2})| \\\\  & ≈0.463\\,6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the point $P$ lies in the second quadrant, we subtract the reference angle from $\\pi ,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\theta  & =\\pi -{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & ≈\\pi -0.463\\,6 \\\\  & ≈2.7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the polar coordinates of $P$ are  $(\\sqrt{5}\\text{ }\\,a,2.7).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265559 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6b04084c-8e69-5f53-a95d-664aba54aec9" :knowledge-point/title "Converting from Cartesian Coordinates to Polar Coordinates: Second or Third Quadrant"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "41d139b2-6fbe-5daf-85ce-183a138bb330" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\sqrt{2}\\text{ }\\,a,\\frac{3\\pi }{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265561} {:answer/id #uuid "710fe7a3-5a61-559f-a993-8bc07b2ff771" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\sqrt{2}\\text{ }\\,a,\\frac{5\\pi }{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265562} {:answer/id #uuid "7d5f0b60-c632-54a3-a69c-9a56a79af719" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\sqrt{2}\\text{ }\\,a,\\frac{7\\pi }{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265563} {:answer/id #uuid "b01f3f79-78a3-5198-a1c8-70acbda225ad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2\\text{ }\\,a,\\frac{3\\pi }{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265564} {:answer/id #uuid "7b27ffab-8c65-53a8-a180-8e9ced20292c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2\\text{ }\\,a,\\frac{5\\pi }{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265565}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "41d139b2-6fbe-5daf-85ce-183a138bb330" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\sqrt{2}\\text{ }\\,a,\\frac{3\\pi }{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265561} :answer-field/id #uuid "714f8f4c-e93f-56b2-a2eb-dca6f43bf78c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265560}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "af8cddb8-79ba-5141-8666-2f47786f56d3" :question/math-academy-id "q-120243" :question/problem "A point $P$ has Cartesian coordinates $(-a,a),$ where $a$ is a positive real number. What are the polar coordinates of $P?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we determine $r:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-a)^{2}+{a}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2{a}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}\\text{ }\\,a.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the reference angle, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\theta }_{R} & =|\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{y}{x})| \\\\  & =|\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{a}{-a})| \\\\  & =|-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(1)| \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the point $P$ lies in the second quadrant, we subtract the reference angle from $\\pi ,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\theta  & =\\pi -{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & =\\pi -\\frac{\\pi }{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\pi }{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the polar coordinates of $P$ are  $(\\sqrt{2}\\text{ }\\,a,\\frac{3\\pi }{4}).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265566 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "49bf1f94-d992-511d-9016-d471f2ad4358" :knowledge-point/title "Converting from Cartesian Coordinates to Polar Coordinates for a Point on an Axis"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "10c09316-c4b6-5f7a-a33b-72d53eaba5ab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3,\\pi )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265568} {:answer/id #uuid "abef886a-c1cd-5f1b-b973-078c2f6b1f6f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3,\\frac{\\pi }{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265569} {:answer/id #uuid "2608ab25-8eda-505e-b00f-3c8e767d5927" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(9,\\pi )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265570} {:answer/id #uuid "56424e8c-a85e-5088-b767-4a0d07a93919" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265571} {:answer/id #uuid "b73e2f18-2596-5d95-aa20-2b17c89ee516" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\sqrt{3},\\frac{\\pi }{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265572}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "56424e8c-a85e-5088-b767-4a0d07a93919" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265571} :answer-field/id #uuid "46b4a0c4-dd26-50d1-b52c-441ffeb39a2a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265567}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "38bbc114-45b7-5162-ba3e-d53e4472f1a9" :question/math-academy-id "q-120295" :question/problem "A point $P$ has Cartesian coordinates $(3,0).$ What are the polar coordinates of $P?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we determine $r:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{3}^{2}+{0}^{2}} \\\\  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the point $P$ lies on the positive half of the $x$-axis, we have $\\theta =0.$ \n\nTherefore, the polar coordinates of $P$ are $(3,0).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265573 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "15b6b8a0-8e4b-51b5-b992-659f82730241" :knowledge-point/title "Converting from Cartesian Coordinates to Polar Coordinates: First or Fourth Quadrant"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c049bebc-5bc0-52cf-8280-4eb903a051ae" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(5\\sqrt{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265575} {:answer/id #uuid "5466f499-7756-553c-a0f9-9f8280cd1d06" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2\\sqrt{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265576} {:answer/id #uuid "bd88312f-145b-587d-8d04-3195603fd3aa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(5\\sqrt{2},\\frac{\\pi }{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265577} {:answer/id #uuid "1771e03a-21a3-5c25-84cd-2c33ea3d21b9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\sqrt{2},\\frac{\\pi }{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265578} {:answer/id #uuid "3c547a7b-2d33-578f-bef2-7c7e1ae864da" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2\\sqrt{5},\\frac{\\pi }{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265579}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bd88312f-145b-587d-8d04-3195603fd3aa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(5\\sqrt{2},\\frac{\\pi }{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265577} :answer-field/id #uuid "ba99f024-7170-580a-9d00-7956533cd3c7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265574}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ca36fa0c-499b-5c42-b666-b6ee1203f754" :question/math-academy-id "q-16951" :question/problem "A point $P$ has Cartesian coordinates $(5,5).$ What are the polar coordinates of $P?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we determine $r:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{5}^{2}+{5}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2\\cdot {5}^{2}} \\\\  & =5\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since the point $P$ lies in the first quadrant, we can determine $\\theta $ by taking the arctangent:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\theta  & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{y}{x}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{5}{5}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(1) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the polar coordinates of $P$ are $(5\\sqrt{2},\\frac{\\pi }{4}).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265580 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "15b6b8a0-8e4b-51b5-b992-659f82730241" :knowledge-point/title "Converting from Cartesian Coordinates to Polar Coordinates: First or Fourth Quadrant"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9fafc44b-38de-57a9-84f6-0c379a41f1c3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1,\\frac{\\pi }{6})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265582} {:answer/id #uuid "37eb47e2-0cb3-54c6-97e2-2f1809ba5e3f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(4,\\frac{\\pi }{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265583} {:answer/id #uuid "d6f39478-4629-5789-b359-4891f1c44b21" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(4,\\frac{\\pi }{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265584} {:answer/id #uuid "64e8d33c-a484-554e-9f8e-2a95e5b36f8f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2,\\frac{\\pi }{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265585} {:answer/id #uuid "4923b131-518a-5941-8845-2a5d9c7767fb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2,\\frac{\\pi }{6})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265586}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "64e8d33c-a484-554e-9f8e-2a95e5b36f8f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2,\\frac{\\pi }{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265585} :answer-field/id #uuid "aaf8873b-a4da-5a70-81b8-f19d046e93d7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265581}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1f794804-51bc-548c-aeae-ca42b3859694" :question/math-academy-id "q-16953" :question/problem "A point $P$ has Cartesian coordinates $(1,\\sqrt{3}).$ What are the polar coordinates of $P?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we determine $r:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+(\\sqrt{3})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+3} \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since the point $P$ lies in the first quadrant, we can determine $\\theta $ by taking the arctangent:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\theta  & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{y}{x}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{1}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\sqrt{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the polar coordinates of $P$ are $(2,\\frac{\\pi }{3}).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265587 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "15b6b8a0-8e4b-51b5-b992-659f82730241" :knowledge-point/title "Converting from Cartesian Coordinates to Polar Coordinates: First or Fourth Quadrant"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5c1f282f-349e-59be-8dd8-73be73e7ba0b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265589} {:answer/id #uuid "746707a3-7ccb-519c-9415-7c6119a84044" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265590}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5c1f282f-349e-59be-8dd8-73be73e7ba0b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265589} :answer-field/id #uuid "7aa62196-bd64-54d0-acd7-3633862596df" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265588} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c3acf709-fc04-58cb-b5a0-8557b5529c4d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{11\\pi}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265592}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c3acf709-fc04-58cb-b5a0-8557b5529c4d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{11\\pi}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265592} :answer-field/id #uuid "9120d48a-f8c9-5f5d-bdbd-fcae34e1dcfb" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265591}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "8110ff45-2d78-5e50-8037-bbc031f60791" :question/math-academy-id "q-275804" :question/problem "A point $P$ has Cartesian coordinates $(\\sqrt{3}k,-k),$ where $k$ is a positive real number. Find the polar coordinates of $P$ expressed in terms of $k.$ Give the polar angle in radians and do not round the answer.\n\nExpressed in terms of $k,$ the polar coordinates of $P$ are $(${{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we determine $r:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(\\sqrt{3}k)^{2}+(-k)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3{k}^{2}+{k}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4{k}^{2}} \\\\  & =2k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe then compute the reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\theta }_{R} & =|\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{y}{x})| \\\\  & =|\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{-k}{\\sqrt{3}k})| \\\\  & =|\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}})| \\\\  & =|-\\frac{\\pi }{6}| \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the point lies in the fourth quadrant, we subtract the reference angle from $2\\pi ,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\theta  & =2\\pi -{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & =2\\pi -\\frac{\\pi }{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{11\\pi }{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the polar coordinates of $P$ are $(2k,\\frac{11\\pi }{6}).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265593 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "15b6b8a0-8e4b-51b5-b992-659f82730241" :knowledge-point/title "Converting from Cartesian Coordinates to Polar Coordinates: First or Fourth Quadrant"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "07ecc3e1-1417-516c-8150-cae5b8507e33" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265595} {:answer/id #uuid "0965b553-7434-5ee8-94de-40a576336554" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265596}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "07ecc3e1-1417-516c-8150-cae5b8507e33" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265595} :answer-field/id #uuid "ea8dbebc-50a6-5da5-b595-0e5ccda7cb02" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265594} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7f014641-1ab4-576a-8ead-e97c21c3f781" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265598}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7f014641-1ab4-576a-8ead-e97c21c3f781" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265598} :answer-field/id #uuid "d34a4681-b904-55b9-8250-71e0adb94ad3" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265597}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "3c2452af-3e76-5807-8b1a-9168b708a216" :question/math-academy-id "q-276599" :question/problem "A point $P$ has Cartesian coordinates $(\\sqrt{2}t,\\sqrt{2}t),$ where $t$ is a positive real number. Find the polar coordinates of $P$ expressed in terms of $t.$ Give the polar angle in radians and do not round the answer.\n\nExpressed in terms of $t,$ the polar coordinates of $P$ are $(${{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we determine $r:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(\\sqrt{2}t)^{2}+(\\sqrt{2}t)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2{t}^{2}+2{t}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4{t}^{2}} \\\\  & =2t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since the point $P$ lies in the first quadrant, we can determine $\\theta $ by taking the arctangent:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\theta  & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{y}{x}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}t}{\\sqrt{2}t}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(1) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the polar coordinates of $P$ are $(2t,\\frac{\\pi }{4}).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265599 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6b04084c-8e69-5f53-a95d-664aba54aec9" :knowledge-point/title "Converting from Cartesian Coordinates to Polar Coordinates: Second or Third Quadrant"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6e0d09bc-643f-5a06-9bf6-19c6795f03cb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265601} {:answer/id #uuid "42524f50-3798-5c02-9707-3079a614972f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265602}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6e0d09bc-643f-5a06-9bf6-19c6795f03cb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265601} :answer-field/id #uuid "7ea287ea-59b3-5839-8857-f5ba2920ebce" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265600} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0d24f85a-acec-5775-806e-303725f73fde" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7\\pi}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265604}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0d24f85a-acec-5775-806e-303725f73fde" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7\\pi}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265604} :answer-field/id #uuid "7277c5d7-ba6e-5ba2-8246-5e6884b3a8b8" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265603}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d944bd6d-9604-537e-9d26-1e1c84591ba5" :question/math-academy-id "q-276605" :question/problem "A point $P$ has Cartesian coordinates $(-\\sqrt{3},-1).$ Find the polar coordinates of $P.$ Give the polar angle in radians and do not round the answer.\n\nThe polar coordinates of $P$ are $(${{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we determine $r:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-\\sqrt{3})^{2}+(-1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3+1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4} \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the reference angle, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\theta }_{R} & =|\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{y}{x})| \\\\  & =|\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{-1}{-\\sqrt{3}})| \\\\  & =|\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}})| \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the point $P$ lies in the third quadrant, we add the reference angle to $\\pi ,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\theta  & =\\pi +{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & =\\pi +\\frac{\\pi }{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{7\\pi }{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the polar coordinates of $P$ are  $(2,\\frac{7\\pi }{6}).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265605 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "49bf1f94-d992-511d-9016-d471f2ad4358" :knowledge-point/title "Converting from Cartesian Coordinates to Polar Coordinates for a Point on an Axis"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0e2572c6-7c82-5295-8151-f07d75c05ff1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265607}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0e2572c6-7c82-5295-8151-f07d75c05ff1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265607} :answer-field/id #uuid "942516fe-7e20-5624-98c1-90eb822f6a2c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265606} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b7268b0a-c1a6-56f4-a062-87110f928f16" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265609}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b7268b0a-c1a6-56f4-a062-87110f928f16" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265609} :answer-field/id #uuid "07dcaada-0df0-515e-9ad8-b9b90a8ecbae" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265608}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "960c6a46-ee9c-52d3-87a8-55a204c2b666" :question/math-academy-id "q-276621" :question/problem "A point $P$ has Cartesian coordinates $(-1,0).$ Find the polar coordinates of $P.$ Give the polar angle in radians and do not round the answer.\n\nThe polar coordinates of $P$ are $(${{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we determine $r:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-1)^{2}+{0}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1} \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the point $P$ lies on the negative half of the $x$-axis, we have $\\theta =\\pi .$ \n\nTherefore, the polar coordinates of $P$ are $(1,\\pi ).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265610 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "49bf1f94-d992-511d-9016-d471f2ad4358" :knowledge-point/title "Converting from Cartesian Coordinates to Polar Coordinates for a Point on an Axis"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e7525df2-c141-5818-bdb1-4c7ab811d214" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265612}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e7525df2-c141-5818-bdb1-4c7ab811d214" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265612} :answer-field/id #uuid "6d854bfe-fb1e-5ab3-b5fd-1ed21432b998" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265611} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4f1fe94f-842f-52b7-9dfa-309729c871c5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265614}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4f1fe94f-842f-52b7-9dfa-309729c871c5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265614} :answer-field/id #uuid "5c8af116-7fcf-5e1a-9d75-ceebb8caa850" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265613}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f6b2924c-4a43-5150-be50-d25fc9f9ce79" :question/math-academy-id "q-276625" :question/problem "A point $P$ has Cartesian coordinates $(0,2).$ Find the polar coordinates of $P.$ Give the polar angle in radians and do not round the answer.\n\nThe polar coordinates of $P$ are $(${{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we determine $r:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{0}^{2}+{2}^{2}} \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the point $P$ lies on the positive half of the $y$-axis, we have $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$ \n\nTherefore, the polar coordinates of $P$ are $(2,\\frac{\\pi }{2}).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265615 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "49bf1f94-d992-511d-9016-d471f2ad4358" :knowledge-point/title "Converting from Cartesian Coordinates to Polar Coordinates for a Point on an Axis"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b58f18d8-fdb2-539b-b42a-56ed36161ddc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265617}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b58f18d8-fdb2-539b-b42a-56ed36161ddc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265617} :answer-field/id #uuid "e62fab08-2dc5-5ec9-843c-87b66e47feb4" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265616} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cb8a6822-1b8e-58f6-874d-d00d1ae77ca5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\pi}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265619}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cb8a6822-1b8e-58f6-874d-d00d1ae77ca5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\pi}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265619} :answer-field/id #uuid "9c9276df-2eac-55c0-b95c-e1154673193e" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265618}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a3d987fd-ebb7-5333-a1be-7b822694cfbb" :question/math-academy-id "q-276627" :question/problem "A point $P$ has Cartesian coordinates $(0,-3).$ Find the polar coordinates of $P.$ Give the polar angle in radians and do not round the answer.\n\nThe polar coordinates of $P$ are $(${{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we determine $r:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{0}^{2}+(-3)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9} \\\\  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the point $P$ lies on the negative half of the $y$-axis, we have $\\theta =\\frac{3\\pi }{2}.$ \n\nTherefore, the polar coordinates of $P$ are $(3,\\frac{3\\pi }{2}).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265620 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6b04084c-8e69-5f53-a95d-664aba54aec9" :knowledge-point/title "Converting from Cartesian Coordinates to Polar Coordinates: Second or Third Quadrant"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1e10b965-c925-53d8-b0a1-e94d02176e02" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{\\sqrt{5}\\,k}{4},2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265622} {:answer/id #uuid "7bef3587-125b-5179-80a3-80f4414e3e9e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{\\sqrt{5}\\,k}{2},1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265623} {:answer/id #uuid "63073362-c098-521f-98fb-ed4cb88c3dad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{\\sqrt{5}\\,k}{2},2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265624} {:answer/id #uuid "b6a61183-5d39-530a-95dd-f751a4bf0422" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{5k}{4},-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265625} {:answer/id #uuid "405bc0fa-9ee4-56e2-9769-0d67981d3e30" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{k}{4},-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265626}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1e10b965-c925-53d8-b0a1-e94d02176e02" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{\\sqrt{5}\\,k}{4},2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265622} :answer-field/id #uuid "33f3924e-2cee-53c6-a9aa-06062e98c158" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265621}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "20c89bd4-10e9-501a-b277-f64d8841ef95" :question/math-academy-id "q-40762" :question/problem "A point $P$ has Cartesian coordinates $(-\\frac{k}{4},\\frac{k}{2}),$ where $k$ is a positive real number. What are the polar coordinates of $P?$ Round your answer to the nearest integer where necessary." :question/worked-solution "First, we determine $r:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{(-\\frac{k}{4})}^{2}+{(\\frac{k}{2})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{{k}^{2}}{16}+\\frac{{k}^{2}}{4}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5\\cdot \\frac{{k}^{2}}{16}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{5}\\,k}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the reference angle, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\theta }_{R} & =|\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{y}{x})| \\\\  & =|\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{(\\frac{k}{2})}{(-\\frac{k}{4})})| \\\\  & =|\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(-2)| \\\\  & ≈1.107.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the point $P$ lies in the second quadrant, we subtract the reference angle from $\\pi $ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\theta  & =\\pi -{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & =\\pi -1.107 \\\\  & ≈2.035 \\\\  & ≈2,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest integer. Therefore, the polar coordinates of $P$ are $(\\frac{\\sqrt{5}\\,k}{4},2).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265627 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "15b6b8a0-8e4b-51b5-b992-659f82730241" :knowledge-point/title "Converting from Cartesian Coordinates to Polar Coordinates: First or Fourth Quadrant"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "efcfb6bc-6285-5d7d-a916-7d7a87fe4834" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(4.12,0.04)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265629} {:answer/id #uuid "186caec4-6366-5e3a-90a9-1807d755ee20" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(4.32,6.21)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265630} {:answer/id #uuid "3b61257e-f8b8-5bd1-a42d-a57082134790" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(4.32,6.04)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265631} {:answer/id #uuid "8efd6dfc-dd36-5f94-83cd-2141f9475e2b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(4.12,6.21)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265632} {:answer/id #uuid "9e96f338-922c-527c-a818-d7beb672d0d7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(4.12,6.04)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265633}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9e96f338-922c-527c-a818-d7beb672d0d7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(4.12,6.04)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265633} :answer-field/id #uuid "e8b6ff64-4f81-5177-a654-ba63c39a0591" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265628}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d77ee1bc-6c25-5c58-accf-40384b1c372b" :question/math-academy-id "q-8511" :question/problem "A point $P$ has Cartesian coordinates $(4,-1).$ What are the polar coordinates of $P?$ Round your answer to two decimal places where necessary." :question/worked-solution "First, we determine $r:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{4}^{2}+{(-1)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{16+1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{17} \\\\  & =4.123\\,10… \\\\  & ≈4.12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe then compute the reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\theta }_{R} & =|\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{y}{x})| \\\\  & =|\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{-1}{4})| \\\\  & =|\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(-0.25)| \\\\  & =|-0.244\\,97…| \\\\  & ≈0.244\\,97\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the point lies in the fourth quadrant, we subtract the reference angle from $2\\pi ,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\theta  & =2\\pi -{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & =2\\pi -0.244\\,97 \\\\  & ≈6.04\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the polar coordinates of $P$ are $(4.12,6.04),$ rounded to two decimal places."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265634 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "49bf1f94-d992-511d-9016-d471f2ad4358" :knowledge-point/title "Converting from Cartesian Coordinates to Polar Coordinates for a Point on an Axis"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c59b21e9-7873-5b17-bb4e-2f03f3dd0778" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(4,\\frac{\\pi }{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265636} {:answer/id #uuid "11587c23-b944-503f-8191-9eb92bf1a2d5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(4,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265637} {:answer/id #uuid "51a17799-128f-5ba1-b78d-fe5f712fc284" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265638} {:answer/id #uuid "0bd99877-895e-534c-a409-ab9d2ba704e6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2,\\frac{\\pi }{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265639} {:answer/id #uuid "a84be9de-52bc-568c-97b2-ce85accb58e2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(4,\\pi )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265640}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "11587c23-b944-503f-8191-9eb92bf1a2d5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(4,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265637} :answer-field/id #uuid "cd35a221-9db6-5604-b45f-260c95060f54" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265635}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "19fb05c5-dc82-5eb3-b084-2c0a8fc41b6a" :question/math-academy-id "q-86616" :question/problem "A point $P$ has Cartesian coordinates $(4,0).$ What are the polar coordinates of $P?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we determine $r:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{4}^{2}+{0}^{2}} \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the point $P$ lies on the positive half of the $x$-axis, we have $\\theta =0.$ \n\nTherefore, the polar coordinates of $P$ are $(4,0).$"}]}
